CAT = "caplumbCommon"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("Before you work on a circulator's wiring, what must you do first?",
 "Lock out and tag the disconnect", ["Turn the thermostat right down", "Close the isolation valves", "Switch it off at the control"],
 "Lockout and tagging at the energy-isolating device is the only way to be sure no one can re-energize the circuit while you work. A thermostat or control switch can be turned back on by someone else."),
("Which document gives the hazards, PPE and first aid for a solvent cement?",
 "The safety data sheet", ["The supplier invoice", "The product warranty", "The drawing schedule"],
 "Under WHMIS, the safety data sheet lists hazards, safe handling, PPE and first aid measures. The supplier label summarizes them, but the SDS is the full reference."),
("Before entering a sewage sump that is a confined space, what must be done first?",
 "Test the atmosphere", ["Open the lid for a minute", "Put on a dust mask", "Lower a light inside"],
 "Confined spaces can hold toxic gases such as hydrogen sulphide or too little oxygen. The air must be tested before entry and monitored while someone is inside, under the provincial confined space rules."),
("What oxygen level is generally considered safe for confined space entry?",
 "19.5% to 23%", ["16% to 19%", "23% to 25%", "12.5% to 16.5%"],
 "Most Canadian OHS rules treat 19.5% to 23% oxygen as acceptable. Below that the air is oxygen-deficient; above it, fire risk rises sharply. Exact limits are set provincially."),
("An extension ladder must reach a landing 4 m up. About how far out should its base be?",
 "About 1 m", ["About 2 m", "About 0.5 m", "About 1.5 m"],
 "The 4 to 1 rule puts the base 1 unit out for every 4 units of height, so 4 m of height needs about 1 m out from the wall."),
("How far should a ladder extend above the landing it is used to reach?",
 "About 1 m", ["About 300 mm", "About 2 m", "Level with the landing"],
 "A ladder used to reach a landing should extend about 1 m (3 ft) above it so the worker has something to hold while stepping on and off."),
("Which way should a pipe wrench be pulled on a fitting?",
 "Toward the jaw opening", ["Away from the jaw opening", "Either way, with a cheater", "Back and forth to seat it"],
 "The hook jaw bites only when the wrench is turned toward the jaw opening. Pulled the other way it slips, and a cheater bar can break the wrench."),
("After cutting steel pipe with a wheel cutter, why is the end reamed?",
 "To remove the inside burr", ["To taper the outside end", "To harden the pipe end", "To square up the end of the cut"],
 "A wheel cutter rolls a burr into the bore, which restricts flow and catches debris. Reaming removes it; squaring is the cutter's job, not the reamer's."),
("A drawing shows piping in three dimensions using 30-degree lines. What type of drawing is it?",
 "An isometric view", ["A plan view", "An elevation view", "A cross-section view"],
 "Isometric drawings show horizontal runs at 30 degrees and verticals as vertical lines, so a whole piping system can be shown in one view. Plans and elevations are flat, single-direction views."),
("On a drawing at a scale of 1:50, a pipe run measures 64 mm. How long is the actual run?",
 "3.2 m", ["6.4 m", "32 m", "1.28 m"],
 "At 1:50, every millimetre on paper is 50 mm on site: 64 x 50 = 3200 mm, or 3.2 m."),
("A pipe passes through a rated fire separation. What is required at the penetration?",
 "A listed firestop system", ["A larger sleeve only", "Expanding foam sealant", "A metal escutcheon plate"],
 "Penetrations of fire separations must be sealed with a tested, listed firestop system that suits the pipe material. Ordinary foam or a cover plate does not restore the rating."),
("Who decides when a plumbing permit and inspection are required?",
 "The local authority", ["The manufacturer", "The contractor", "The Red Seal board"],
 "Permits and inspections are set by the province or territory and the local authority having jurisdiction, so the rules vary. The Red Seal program certifies trades people; it does not issue permits."),
("Where should excavated soil be placed beside an open trench?",
 "Back from the edge", ["Right at the trench edge", "In the trench bottom", "On the pipe once laid"],
 "Spoil piled at the edge adds weight that can collapse the wall and can roll back onto workers. Provincial OHS rules set the minimum distance, commonly about 1 m."),
("You have just brazed near wood framing. What is good practice before leaving?",
 "Keep a fire watch on it", ["Spray water into the wall", "Leave once the joint cools", "Wrap the joint in tape"],
 "Smouldering framing can ignite well after the torch is off, so hot work rules call for a fire watch after the work ends. The required time varies by site and province."),
("When teaching an apprentice a new task, what step follows your demonstration?",
 "Let them practise it", ["Give a written test", "Move to a new task", "Have them watch it"],
 "Effective trade mentoring follows demonstrate, then let the learner practise, then give feedback. Watching again or testing on paper skips the hands-on step where skill is built."),
("A supervisor gives you a verbal change to a pipe route. What is the best way to confirm it?",
 "Repeat it back to confirm", ["Assume you heard it right", "Ask another apprentice", "Wait for new drawings"],
 "Repeating the instruction back catches misunderstandings before pipe is cut. Record the change too, so the as-built drawings can be updated."),
("What is a material takeoff used for?",
 "Listing materials to order", ["Scheduling the site inspections", "Recording as-built changes", "Testing the pipe pressure"],
 "A takeoff counts pipe, fittings and supplies from the drawings and specifications so material can be ordered and the job planned."),
("What do as-built drawings record?",
 "Where pipe was really run", ["The first design only", "The inspector's site notes", "The supplier's prices"],
 "As-builts show the system as actually installed, including field changes, so future service work can find valves, cleanouts and buried lines."),
("Two 90-degree elbows are 900 mm centre to centre. Each has a 25 mm fitting allowance. What is the pipe's cut length?",
 "850 mm", ["875 mm", "900 mm", "950 mm"],
 "Subtract the fitting allowance (take-off) of both fittings from the centre-to-centre measurement: 900 - 25 - 25 = 850 mm."),
("An offset of 600 mm is made with two 45-degree fittings. What is the travel, centre to centre?",
 "About 850 mm", ["About 600 mm", "About 690 mm", "About 1570 mm"],
 "For 45-degree fittings, travel = offset x 1.414, so 600 x 1.414 = about 848 mm. 690 mm uses the 60-degree constant (1.155) and 1570 mm the 22.5-degree one (2.613)."),
("A 12 m horizontal drain is graded at 1 in 50. How much fall does it need?",
 "240 mm", ["120 mm", "24 mm", "600 mm"],
 "A slope of 1 in 50 is 2%, so the fall is 12 000 mm / 50 = 240 mm."),
("A storage tank's water level is 10 m above a tap. What is the static pressure at the tap?",
 "About 98 kPa", ["About 10 kPa", "About 14 kPa", "About 196 kPa"],
 "Each metre of water column gives about 9.81 kPa, so 10 m gives about 98 kPa. 14 is the answer in psi, not kPa."),
("Who should give hand signals to a crane operator during a lift?",
 "One designated signaller", ["Anyone near the load", "Each member of the rigging crew", "Whoever sees a problem"],
 "Only one designated signaller directs the operator, so signals never conflict. The one exception is an emergency stop, which anyone may give."),
("Cutting a concrete floor for a new drain creates silica dust. What is the best control?",
 "Wet cutting with extraction", ["A paper nuisance mask", "Working faster to finish", "Dry sweeping up the dust after"],
 "Respirable crystalline silica causes silicosis. Engineering controls such as water suppression or a vacuum shroud come first, with a fitted respirator where needed; dry sweeping puts the dust back in the air."),
]
